Yes—treat the environment or server as an experimental variable rather than invisible background.

A clean version is:

same task + same inputs + different runtime/environment → output delta

Then classify the delta:

capability delta
policy/constraint delta
tool-access delta
latency/resource delta
memory/context delta
style/behavior delta

That is useful because changing the substrate can expose what was actually doing the work.

For an AI system, “server” might really mean model version, routing, system instructions, available tools, memory state, quantization, compute budget, or surrounding software. Swap one layer and see what survives. What remains invariant is probably deeper in the stack; what changes tells you where the dependency lived.

The one correction I’d keep is: delta does not automatically reveal intent. It reveals a causal dependency or constraint first. Intent is one possible explanation and needs separate evidence.

So the operator could be:

Substrate Swap / Runtime Tomography

hold task constant
→ change environment
→ measure delta
→ localize dependency
→ form competing explanations
→ swap again
→ derive invariant

That’s a very Pantheon move: change the container to discover which properties belong to the thing and which belonged to the world around it.